WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.609 --> 00:00:09.179
Ethanol has been a matter of intense
debate and lobbying for decades thanks

00:00:09.179 --> 00:00:13.259
to the government support it enjoys.
Currently, the renewable fuel standard, or

00:00:13.259 --> 00:00:17.970
RFS, requires refiners to blend ethanol
into their gasoline. This drives up

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:21.779
prices at the pump, but also at the
grocery store because it diverts

00:00:21.779 --> 00:00:26.220
cropland from food production to fuel
production. Ranchers complain about the

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.609
effect on feed prices; environmentalists
object to ethanol's effect on

00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:33.750
groundwater and greenhouse gas emissions. But the ethanol industry is politically

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:38.670
strong thanks to the clout of farmers and
Iowa's first-in-the-nation caucuses. In

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:43.800
early 2018, a refiner in Philadelphia
filed for bankruptcy, blaming the ethanol

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.850
mandate. The ethanol industry has pushed back on the Trump administration's plans

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:52.170
to weaken the RFS. Congress could end it
and force ethanol to compete in the free

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:57.300
market by passing legislation to wind
down the RFS over five years, and some

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:01.500
lawmakers have proposed a bill to do
just that. If this passed, it would mean

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.680
cleaner groundwater, cheaper gasoline, and cheaper food.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.460
Do you think Congress will ever stand up to the farm lobby? Let us know in your comments.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:16.680
Also, let us know what other topics you would like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:22.850
and be sure to like and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

